item.text:
{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q005_cupredoxin_function_transfer","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 supports probable common ancestry among these domains despite their different biological roles. It places them in three distinct families: Plastocyanin/azurin-like, b.6.1.1 (SUNID 49504); Ephrin ectodomain, b.6.1.5 (74874); and Peptidylarginine deiminase PAD4, N-terminal domain, b.6.1.6 (110107). All share the Cupredoxins superfamily, b.6.1 (49503), within the Cupredoxin-like fold, b.6 (49502), and All beta proteins class, b (48724). The shared superfamily supports an evolutionary relationship; fold and class alone would establish only structural similarity. The fold annotation describes a Greek-key beta sandwich with seven strands in two sheets, with additional strands in some members.

Copper binding and electron transfer should not be transferred across these families. Although the superfamily carries the general comment “contains copper-binding site,” the ephrin and PAD4 N-terminal family annotations explicitly say they are probably related to cupredoxins but lack the metal-binding site. Those specific annotations prevent treating the superfamily name or general comment as a universal functional property. Canonical plastocyanin and azurin are copper electron carriers; ephrin ectodomains serve signaling functions, and the PAD4 assignment concerns its N-terminal domain, not the function of the entire enzyme or its catalytic domain.

A copper-binding claim would require target-specific conservation and spatial arrangement of coordinating residues, ligand-bound structural or spectroscopic evidence, and biochemical metal-binding evidence. Electron transfer additionally requires redox and electron-transfer measurements and appropriate physiological partners. Sequence and structural alignments could substantiate the remote relationship, but the retrieved hierarchy does not itself provide those alignments. Shared ancestry therefore does not establish identical function or interchangeable functional templates.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","Pretrained biological knowledge","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ephrin&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=PAD4&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/b.6.1.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/b.6.1.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49504","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49504/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49504/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49504/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/74874","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/74874/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/74874/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/74874/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110107","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110107/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110107/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110107/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49503/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49502","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49502/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49502/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/49502/homology"]}]}
